  "Question": "When negating “There exists \\(x\\in\\mathbb{R}\\) such that \\(x^2<0\\),” what should the quantifier become?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "It remains an existential quantifier",
    "B": "Remove the quantifier",
    "C": "It becomes two existential quantifiers",
    "D": "A universal quantifier"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "The negation of an existential quantifier is a universal quantifier. The original statement is false, and its negation, “The square of every real number is greater than or equal to 0,” is true.",
